## STILL MISSING

### SIMPLICITY is stated per-subgroup, because there is no sort of subgroups

`isSimpleWitness` in `std/group/normal.b4m` is `isTrivialSubgroup or isTheWholeGroup` — the
property of THE subgroup that file carries, not "for all normal subgroups N of G".
Quantifying over subgroups needs a sort of subgroups, the same limit Chapter 9 hit for ≅
(CHAPTER9-PAINPOINTS: "2b4m can state a theory and MODEL one theory in another, but cannot
talk about theories as objects").

The workable shape is the one std already uses: a consumer declares its own membership
predicate and proves the disjunction for it. `std/subgroup.b4m` has a second predicate
`inSubgroup2` for exactly this reason (two subgroups at once), which is the
declare-what-you-need pattern rather than a limitation.

### An UNINSTANTIATED SCHEMA reports "0 theorems proven" and nothing else

Not an error, not a warning. Re-probed 2026-11-05: a file whose only content is a schema
exits 0 with `OK: 2 declarations, 0 theorems proven`. A schema with no instantiation is
unverified and SILENT about it.

This cost cycles twice in this chapter — once on a typo'd step label in the capstone that
checked green until the instantiation existed, once on a probe I believed had validated a
leaf when it had checked nothing. It is the strongest argument for `--library`'s rule that
every schema be exercised where it is declared. Also recorded in CHAPTER5-PAINPOINTS.

### `disequalityIsSymmetric` exists THREE TIMES, once per sort, with no home

    peano_order.natDisequalityIsSymmetric        a != b -> b != a over Nat
    alternating.disequalityIsSymmetric           over Element
    decomposition.pointDisequalityIsSymmetric    over Element, AGAIN

The same four-step proof. The Element pair is duplicated because `alternating.b4m` imports
`decomposition.b4m`, so the lower file cannot cite the higher one's copy. The obvious home is
`std/element.b4m`, which is deliberately axiom-free — but this is a THEOREM over an
unconstrained sort, so it needs no axioms, and the "axiom-free" rationale may not actually
forbid it. Worth settling: either put it there, or write down why a sort-generic logical fact
has no home.

`tautology` is why these are needed at all: it sees `a != b` and `b != a` as unrelated opaque
atoms, so disequality symmetry is a real step at every site.

### `at` has the same spelling for Seq and Points, and it bit three times

`std/permutation/decomposition.b4m` aliases `at` to the SEQUENCE version while cycle indexing
needs the `Points` one. Three separate occurrences in that file, each diagnosed instantly
("expected sort 'Seq', got 'Points'") and each costing a round trip. Qualify `cycle_theory.at`
by reflex in anything touching both. The underlying issue is CHAPTER6's: two same-named
things from different origins print identically.

