## WHAT WORKED, worth keeping

### A coset is its MEMBERSHIP PREDICATE, and Judson's equalities survive that

There is no sort of subsets of a group, so §10.1's `gN = Ng` is extensional agreement of two
predicates and `aN = bN` is `inSubgroup(a⁻¹b)` (§6.1's
`cosetsCoincideIffQuotientIsInTheSubgroup`). Both are exactly how Judson USES them — he never
needs the coset as an object, only membership and equality — so the transliteration is
faithful rather than a workaround. `coset.b4m` develops only the left coset (Lagrange needs
one, and the proofs mirror), so `inRightCoset` is defined in `normal.b4m`.

### READ THE SOURCE BEFORE SCOPING — the chapter's central lesson, learned three times

Three times I scoped machinery from the shape of what I had already built instead of from
Judson's text, and all three times the machinery did not exist to be built:

1. **"Lemma 10.10 needs a cycle-type CLASSIFICATION theorem"** — five successive overstated
   gaps, zero survivors of a probe. Every case's proof reads sigma at THREE POINTS; cycle type
   is how Judson FINDS those points, not a hypothesis the algebra consumes.
2. **"Transitivity needs any-triple-to-any-triple, with n >= 5 to repair parity"** — collapsed
   by two probes. Conjugation acts on 3-CYCLES (invariant under rotation), not triples, and
   the uniform conjugator `(a,b,c)` is a 3-cycle, hence even BY CONSTRUCTION. n >= 5 is spent
   in exactly one place: the twist, whose only relabelling is a single swap, needs a spare
   transposition to stay even.
3. **"Branches 2 and 3 need the decomposition's MAXIMUM cycle length, a second induction"** —
   worst in kind, because the source was in the repo (`vendor/aata/src/normal.xml:366-480`)
   and I did not open it. Judson ASSERTS the case list ("There are several possible cycle
   structures for sigma"). Exhaustiveness is the one step of his proof that is a claim rather
   than a computation, so the formalization's boundary is that sentence and everything
   downstream is kernel-checked.

**THE RULE:** when the task is transcription, read the source's own case structure BEFORE
scoping — not after a plan exists, not to check a plan. A plan built from the formalization's
own shape will reliably invent work the author never did, and it will look rigorous while
doing so. A probe tells you whether YOUR idea works; it cannot tell you the author had a
different idea. (`agents/2b4m-cheatsheet.md` already says bottom-up when the statement is
GIVEN — this is the same lesson one level up: the PROOF STRATEGY is given too.)

There is a matching RETRACTION worth keeping. I built a five-theorem "covering proof" —
a free-standing case analysis over cycles — and reported its residue as the chapter's gap. It
was my construction, not Judson's, and it arose from a real win: restating each case over
POINT-VALUES is what made the identities length-free, but point-values decouple the cases from
sigma's SHAPE, so the arms then need a shape-to-point-value conversion Judson never pays for.
Six of nine lemmas were deleted. Two came back, and the distinction is the keeper:

> **A NOTATION BRIDGE between two formal spellings of the same object is permanent
> infrastructure; a RECONSTRUCTION of the source's own reasoning is not.**

The two bridges — `aDisjointRemainderPreservesCycleValues` (sigma's values equal its cycle's,
on that support) and `aThreeLengthCycleIsAThreeCycleProduct` (std's cycle fold equals the
identities' fold) — are steps Judson never writes because on paper the notations denote one
object. Any route through his cases needs them.

### WHAT TRANSCRIBING A TEXTBOOK PROOF ACTUALLY COSTS

Every gap in all four of Judson's Lemma 10.10 cases was a step he compresses into a phrase:

    "sigma = tau(a1...ar)" treating a1..ar as sigma's points  -> the value transfer
    "disjoint cycles"                                          -> cross-cycle disequalities
                                                                  (`distinctEntriesDiffer`
                                                                  cannot do these;
                                                                  `pointsInDisjointSupportsDiffer` can)
    "(a1 a2 a3)"                                               -> the two folds are one object
    "a further conjugation"                                    -> TWO conjugations, the second
                                                                  reading point-values off the
                                                                  FIRST's output, not off sigma

None is a gap in his reasoning; all are places where one notation does work a formalization
must spell out. That is the honest shape of the task.

Two related measurements:

- **The hypothesis set a POINTWISE PROOF needs is larger than the IDENTITY needs.** Case 2's
  identity holds assuming only two disequalities (verified over 2000 assignments); the
  pointwise proof needs all ten, because each trace must know which branch of `transposition`
  applies at every factor. Minimal hypotheses for a TRUTH and for a PROOF differ.
- **An UNUSED hypothesis is rejected, and that is right.** `a != k` was in a statement because
  Judson's context supplies it; the proof never consulted it and the unused-fact check threw
  it out. An invented hypothesis reads as a condition the mathematics requires when it is only
  one the author assumed. Same thing happened to `a != c` in the conjugator lemma — the
  identity holds at a = c too.

### VERIFY COMPUTATIONALLY FIRST, THEN LOOK FOR THE STATEMENT THAT DROPS THE STRUCTURE

The method behind every collapsed gap above. A python trace over the relevant points, run
BEFORE writing any proof, repeatedly turned an r-dependent identity into a length-free closed
form — e.g. `sigma (i,j,a) sigma^-1 (i,j,a)^-1 = T(si,sa) T(si,sj) T(i,j) T(i,a)`, which
depends only on sigma's action at three points, verified exhaustively over all 7200
combinations on five points.

Three traps it caught that would have landed as late, confusing kernel failures:

- **CONJUGATION ORDER.** `sigma^-1 c sigma c^-1` (Judson's) and `sigma c sigma^-1 c^-1` have
  DIFFERENT values. Writing a proof from the wrong trace fails late.
- **GROUPING, not just order.** `sigma^-1 c sigma c^-1` must be read as
  `sigma^-1 * (c sigma c^-1)` — the inner conjugate is in N by normality, then closure under
  inverses and products finish it. The other reading ("conjugate c by sigma^-1") is useless,
  because c is a 3-cycle, not an element of N. Each case regroups DIFFERENTLY: case 1
  conjugates sigma, case 4 step 2 conjugates sigma^INVERSE.
- **FACTOR-ORDER CONVENTION.** std's cycle fold makes `(n0...nk)` equal
  `T(n0,nk) o ... o T(n0,n1)`, outermost first. A trace script listing them the other way
  reported a true identity FALSE at every point. **A trace that fails at EVERY point is far
  more likely to be a convention error than a false identity.**

### PROBE WHETHER AN ACCELERANT ALREADY DOES IT, BEFORE WRITING A HELPER

I wrote `twoFactorValue` / `fourFactorValue` / `sixFactorValue`, giving an N-factor composite's
value at a point. All three were `composeApply` applied a fixed number of times with ONE
parenthesization hardcoded; `twoFactorValue` was `composeApply` verbatim, which std's alias
rule forbids outright. A seventh factor, or the same six grouped differently, would each have
needed another theorem.

    [using simplify_quantified composeApply]   -- for a quantified statement
    [using simplify composeApply]              -- at a concrete call site

Either unfolds ANY nesting of ANY length in one step, including a twelve-factor composite.
Deleting the three helpers and rewriting their 18 call sites removed 123 lines.

In `std/` accelerants are the preferred proof; a hand-written N-ary family is the thing to be
suspicious of.

### A SCHEMA CANNOT RECURSE ON ITS OWN ARGUMENT, and the growing-term form HANGS

- `thm(n)` citing `thm(n)` — correctly rejected, `cyclic schema instantiation`.
- `thm(n)` citing `thm(prev(n))` — NOT rejected, and DIVERGES. `prev(n)` is a syntactically
  distinct term, so each instance gets a distinct name, the cycle check never fires, and the
  argument GROWS (`prev(prev(n))`, …) instead of shrinking. Killed after 2.5 minutes of CPU on
  an 8-declaration file.

A schema is a MACRO monomorphized at the WRITTEN argument, with no evaluation of
`prev(succ(k)) -> k` at instantiation time. Terminating would require the instantiator to
compute — which is what `induction` does, and why `std/peano.b4m`'s `induction` schema exists
and transfers through models.

USER (2026-10-07): curiosity, not a requirement — "it's possible we will never need this."
Recorded because the growing-term form HANGS rather than diagnosing: if a schema ever needs a
depth or term-size cap, this is the motivating case.

### TACTIC BOUNDARIES, each of which cost a debugging round

- **`simplify` needs an EQUATION goal.** Deriving `z != i` from `z != x` and `x = i` looks
  equational; `[using simplify …]` answers `goal is not an equation`. `[by rewrite x-is-i
  z-not-x]` does it — the kernel rule substitutes into any formula, including a disequation,
  and is bidirectional. The assembly needed 32 of these.
- **`simplify` will not REASSOCIATE to expose a cancellation.** `op(c, op(inverse(c), d)) = d`
  fails even with `grpAssoc`, `grpInverseRight` and `grpIdentityLeft` in the set. It rewrites
  to a normal form; it does not search.
- **`assoc(lemma)` is the tactic for regrouping.** Four hand instantiations of `composeAssoc`
  (each aimed by hand, each failing with a whole-term expected-vs-got) collapsed to one
  `[using assoc(composeAssoc)]`, which right-nests BOTH sides and compares. If the step is
  "the same factors, differently grouped", that is the tactic. But `assoc` reassociates a
  product against a product, so it cannot take `cycle(s,5) = <nested>` to a regrouped RHS —
  that regrouping is its own equation, then `chain`.
- **`rewrite` rewrites a FORMULA with an equation; it does not conjure the formula.** From
  `f = compose(g, c)` you cannot reach `invertible(compose(g, cycle(...)))` in one step:
  rewrite the equation into `invertible(f)` first, THEN the cycle's spelling into that result.
  Two steps per fact.
- **STAGE A LONG CHAIN at the orientation boundary.** Nine equations exceeded `chain`'s search
  ("cannot connect …") and one `simplify` failed too (two links must run backward). What
  worked: `simplify` for the seven left-to-right links, then `chain` for the two reversible
  ones. Neither tactic spans the whole distance.
- **`chain` REJECTS a repeated ref** (`chain a b a` is a label-shadowing error) and cannot
  prove a DISEQUATION (that is `[by rewrite eq diseq]`).
- **`arithmetic(peano)` treats `succ` OPAQUELY**, so every index fact with a VARIABLE under
  `succ` comes back "the statement is false" while the ground ones pass. Stating a length as
  `succ^4(q)` rather than `p >= 3` makes every index fact structural instead.
- **`--axioms` does not see inside a `simplify` rewrite set.** Dropping aliases it reported
  unused removed `invProduct`, which IS cited — by a `simplify` step. `--axioms` attributes
  what a proof RESTS on; a rewrite named in a `simplify` set does not appear there.

### `tautology` CANNOT LINK EQUALITY ATOMS — and the countermodel says so exactly

Replacing a nested 4x4 position split with one flat 13-way disjunction failed:

    countermodel: x = i := true, y = j := false, z = j := false,
                  x = j := true, y = i := false, z = i := false

It set `x = i` AND `x = j` both true. With `i != j` in hand that is impossible, but
`tautology` sees `x = i`, `x = j` and `i != j` as three unrelated opaque atoms. Adding
`i != j` as a premise does not help, for the same reason.

The nested split works because each LEVEL is a genuine propositional tautology over its own
atoms, cross-linking nothing. The three same-coordinate clashes then never need ruling out
propositionally — the leaf derives `i = j` by symmetry-and-rewrite and discharges with
`ex_falso`, which is the standard remedy ("do the non-propositional step yourself, then let
tautology close it").

### `specialize` NEEDS TERM ARGS FOR BINDERS THAT FOLLOW A PREMISE

`conjugationIsMultiplicative` is `forall sigma; invertible(sigma) -> forall f, g; …`.
`specialize conjugationIsMultiplicative(sigma) sigma-is-a-permutation` leaves `f` and `g`
UNBOUND, and the diagnostic shows the remainder as `forall b1: Fn; forall b2: Fn; …` —
accurate but easy to misread as a shape mismatch. Put EVERY term in the parens regardless of
where its binder sits. Same rule CHAPTER6-PAINPOINTS records for `fullOrderGenerates`.

Two more citation shapes that cost a cycle each:

- **Inside a `case` arm, the arm label names a SUBPROOF.** `[using tautology <arm-label>]`
  errors ("names a subproof; a step reference is required"); restate the assumption first with
  `[by hypothesis <arm-label>]`.
- **A schema citing a schema instantiates the inner one AT ITS OWN PARAMETER:**
  `[using instantiation innerSchema((fun g: Fn => inQ(g))) refs]`.

### GENERATOR DESIGN: DEMAND-DRIVEN, and precompute the table before branching

Four generator iterations were the SAME defect in different places — **emitting a fact nothing
cites.** `2b4m` rejects that outright ("unused fact"), which made each round a one-line
diagnosis, but the shape is worth recording because a generator invites it: pre-ensuring both
orientations of a disequality when each leaf consumes one; restating all four hypotheses when
leaves cite two; projecting every conjunct when a leaf uses a subset.

A hand-written proof cites what it needs because the author writes the citation first. A
generator emits a SUPPLY and hopes it matches the DEMAND. Making the emitter demand-driven —
body first, facts second, each on request — removed all four at once.

Two companions:

- **When the emitter grows branches for cases that may not arise, compute the answer for every
  case first and LOOK AT THE TABLE.** Two attempts at a recursive disequality solver
  accumulated edge cases; a precomputed table showed 54 steps across 13 arms, every one of
  exactly two forms, and that the hard case (a chain of two substitutions) never occurs.
- **Check brace DEPTH at the discharge steps, not the total count.** Both generators this round
  emitted each discharge INSIDE the block it discharges, and the totals balanced in both broken
  versions.

And **verify the plan mechanically before generating** — four script checks each caught
something that would otherwise have surfaced partway through a 500-line proof, including two
missing leaves. But a plan-verification script WEAKER than the proof system reports gaps that
are not there (one check's first pass substituted in only one direction and falsely flagged 12
of 13 leaves); one STRONGER misses real gaps. Matching it to the available inference is the
whole trick.

### BEFORE STRENGTHENING WHAT FEEDS A PROOF, GREP WHAT THE PROOF CONSUMES

The selector's premise was "N has the family at EVERY pair (i2,j2)", but all 21 of its uses
read `family(i, j, ·)` with i and j its OWN binders. The premise weakened to one pair, `fix
i`/`fix j` moved outside the `assume`, and the 844-line body changed only by dropping a
now-absent disequality per specialize. The all-pairs premise had never been earned; a
weakening that looked like real work was a one-hour frame rewrite.

Two related things Judson supplies silently, each invisible in the prose:

- **A spare point** for the conjugator `(a,b,c)` — the chapter's only use of n >= 5, now
  `set_finite.aSetOfAtLeastFiveHasAFifthPoint`.
- **The DEGENERATE arm.** Where a = b the target IS the source and the move does not apply at
  all.

And the mirror image: **"relabel its points as (1 2 3)" costs exactly nothing.** The 3-cycle's
points being existentially bound, whatever 3-cycle N contains supplies i, j and a itself. The
relabelling that reads like a step is free; the point supply that reads like nothing is the
real cost.

### `Element` IS AN UNCONSTRAINED SORT, so "there are at least five points" has no expression

`std/element.b4m` declares `Element` with no operations and no axioms, deliberately. So every
earlier statement of the n >= 5 hypothesis in this chapter was informal in a way I had not
noticed: the theorems said "given these five distinct points", never "five exist".

std already had the convention and it was not a new mechanism: bound the domain with a SET
carrying `hasSize`, the way `decomposition.finiteSupportDecomposes` bounds a support. Two
theorems in `std/set/finite.b4m` — `aDistinctPointSurvivesARemoval` and
`aSetOfAtLeastFiveHasAFifthPoint` (iterated `removingAnElementDropsTheSize`, then
`sizedSetHasAMember` on the remainder).

**ITERATED REMOVAL BEAT THE SLICKER ROUTE.** `properSubsetIsSmaller` gives a contradiction
argument, but it needs the four-point set BUILT and SIZED (three insertions carrying six
non-membership side conditions) plus a subset proof. Iterated removal needs no set
construction at all. Counted both before writing either.

### A COUNTING MEASUREMENT MUST EXCLUDE WHAT YOU ARE ABOUT TO DELETE

I recommended keeping six scaffolding lemmas on the strength of a citation count showing
`natDisequalityIsSymmetric` with "8 citations". All eight lines were inside the three theorems
I had already decided to delete — a `grep -c` counting uses BY the scaffolding, not by the
library. After deletion all six read one line each: their own declaration.

When counting whether a lemma is load-bearing, exclude the things you are about to remove, or
the scaffolding vouches for itself.

### CHECK WHETHER A CASE IS A CANCELLATION BEFORE REACHING FOR EXTENSIONALITY

Three of Judson's four cases needed a five- or six-case pointwise split. Case 4 step 1 needed
no extensionality and no hypotheses at all: the middle pair `T(a2,a1) T(a1,a2)` is the identity
by symmetry plus the involution law — four algebra steps. Worth the ten seconds to check.

The same move at the statement level, twice: `squaringKillsACommutingInvolution` is pure
composition algebra with no cycles (if the factors commute and the first is an involution,
`(t o g)^2 = g^2`), and stating case 3 that way — on the ALGEBRA its proof uses rather than on
cycle structure — is what made the proof short. A caller holding the structural form discharges
the hypotheses from `disjointPermutationsCommute`.

### I STARTED A PYTHON EMITTER FOUR TIMES AND DELETED IT THREE

Each time a section got long. `no-throwaway-abstractions` is why: the generator is scaffolding
that must be deleted at the end, it encodes the proof shape in a second place, and its output
is unreviewable by the usual means. Twice, RECONSIDERING THE STATEMENT removed the need
entirely — e.g. four shared-point leads that differ only in which transposition argument flips
became one theorem on the NORMALIZED form plus one rewrite per lead, and
`concatenationPreservesTranspositions` (188 lines, one predicate baked in) became
`concatenationPreservesEntryProperty(prop(Fn))` with both versions as one-line instantiations.

If the volume is worth paying, pay it in 2b4m with the cases written out; if it is not, the
honest move is to stop and say so. The fourth time it WAS worth it (13 generated leaves), and
the generator-design lessons above are from that round.

